{"author":{"id":"5698b6d16d951325374257d86a2e673da0e55970c3d21c0f7c6f49018f24e5bf"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1786429287,"nonce":"1q2XFuJv3UsPL19CSxvcnQ2bWzc=","title":"The tuning defaults are spelled in four places and only a test holds them together","message":"**What is wrong.** The lifecycle/tuning defaults exist as four separate spellings:\n\n| role | file |\n|---|---|\n| source | `internal/identity/domain/org.go:201-249` |\n| mirror | `internal/alerts/domain/lifecycle.go:26,29` |\n| mirror | `internal/grouping/domain/damping.go:27-42` |\n| mirror | `internal/alerts/service/deps.go:271-283` |\n\nThey all AGREE today (checked while landing ADR 0026). Two sharp edges make that luck rather than structure:\n\n1. `deps.go:281-282` spells `FlapThreshold: 5` and `FlapWindow: 2 * time.Hour` as **bare literals**, not as `identity.Default*` references. The other mirrors at least name a constant.\n2. The only thing holding the four together is `TestEveryMirroredDefaultAgreesWithIdentity` in `internal/identity/domain/defaults_derivation_test.go`, which uses **test-only cross-package imports** — the packages must not depend on each other at build time. So **the compiler enforces nothing**. Delete or skip that one file and four packages can silently disagree.\n\n**Why it matters.** ADR 0026 moved three of these numbers at once (refire_grace 600→1200, group_close_delay 300→1200, flap_window 1800→7200), and two mirrored copies were missed until the test suite caught them — which is the failure mode arriving on schedule. The consequence of a miss is not a crash: an org whose settings failed to load runs the OLD arithmetic while the rest of the system runs the new, and nobody is told. That is the 'silent second product' the pinning test's own comment warns about.\n\n**Where.** The four files above, plus `internal/identity/domain/defaults_derivation_test.go`.\n\n**Done when.** Either the mirrors are collapsed to one home, or the coupling is made compile-time (e.g. the mirrors are declared as references to the source constants rather than as literals) so that a divergence cannot compile. A test that is the only guard is not a guard.","files":null}]}